---
title: "The story — FrontierJS"
description: "Software isn't one thing. Data, access, integrations, background work, infrastructure, monitoring, testing, releases and tooling — and where each one lives in FrontierJS."
render: static
---

<!--
  Each "In FrontierJS" line names only what exists. A job with no tool yet
  says so in its chapter rather than being left out.
-->
<section id="top" class="hero">
  <div class="container">
    <p class="eyebrow">The story</p>
    <h1>Software isn't one thing.</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      Sure, it's the data and the screens. It's also who gets to see what, the
      email that has to go out, the job that runs at 3am, the server it all
      lives on, knowing when something breaks, and the backup you hope you
      never need. FrontierJS gives every one of those a home.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="chapters">
  <div class="container">
    <ol class="arc">
      <li>
        <span class="n">00</span>
        <div>
          <h2>Why this is hard</h2>
          <p>Each of those jobs usually comes with its own tool, its own config, and its own idea of what a "user" is. That's fine on day one. Then the validator forgets a field, the API docs fall a release behind, and there's a cron job running that nobody remembers writing.</p>
          <p>Every seam between two tools is a place where two versions of the truth can drift apart, and someone has to notice. Usually it's you, and usually at a bad time.</p>
          <p class="takeaway">Every duplicated truth eventually becomes a duplicated mental model.</p>
          <p class="see">See: <a href="/before-after/">Before &amp; after</a></p>
        </div>
      </li>
      <li>
        <span class="n">01</span>
        <div>
          <h2>Start with information</h2>
          <p>Before your app can do anything, it has to know something. What's a Lead? What fields does it have, what does it belong to, and what can't be left blank? You write that once, in <code>db/schema.lite</code>. Declare <code>model Lead</code> and <code>db.lead</code> already works, with no ORM setup and no model class to keep in sync.</p>
          <p class="home">In FrontierJS: Litestone — the <code>.lite</code> language, a SQLite client, migrations diffed from the schema, tenants, and Studio.</p>
          <p class="takeaway">Data is the foundation, not ORM boilerplate.</p>
        </div>
      </li>
      <li>
        <span class="n">02</span>
        <div>
          <h2>Make it useful</h2>
          <p>Now turn that information into something people can use. Junction serves <code>lead</code> as an API straight from the schema. <code>Lead.mesa</code> builds its form from the same definition, and its list updates live over WebSocket.</p>
          <p>Need a marketing site, an embeddable widget, a browser extension, a desktop app or a CLI? Each one is a folder next to <code>api/</code> and <code>web/</code>, and they all work with the same Lead.</p>
          <p class="home">In FrontierJS: Junction (API) · Sierra (routing and build) · Mesa (one component language, compiled for each surface).</p>
          <p class="takeaway">One system, seen through different surfaces.</p>
          <p class="predict">Predict it: why is this field a select?</p>
          <p class="see">See: <a href="/flow/">Flow</a></p>
        </div>
      </li>
      <li>
        <span class="n">03</span>
        <div>
          <h2>Put boundaries around it</h2>
          <p>Data isn't much use if anyone can do anything with it. So who's allowed to see a Lead? Edit one? Change its status? You answer that right on the model: <code>@@gate</code> for who can read and write, <code>@encrypted</code> and <code>@guarded</code> for sensitive fields, and <code>@@transitions</code> for which status changes are allowed.</p>
          <p>Those rules are enforced where the data lives, so no route, job or agent can sneak around them. And the audit trail logs protected fields as <code>[redacted]</code> without you having to ask.</p>
          <p class="home">In FrontierJS: Litestone gates and policies · <code>@frontierjs/auth</code> for sign-in, sessions and API keys.</p>
          <p class="takeaway">Access is declared in the schema, not written into hooks.</p>
          <p class="predict">Predict it: who is allowed to delete a Lead?</p>
        </div>
      </li>
      <li>
        <span class="n">04</span>
        <div>
          <h2>Connect it outward</h2>
          <p>Your app doesn't live alone. Mail has to go out, payments come in, and other systems want to know when something changes. Every outside service you talk to is declared in one place, with its own timeouts and retries, and every call to it goes through <code>app.conduit.send()</code>. When something changes in your app, webhooks go out. They're delivered at least once and retried if the other end is down.</p>
          <p class="home">In FrontierJS: Conduit · Junction's mail and webhook batteries · raw routes for a callback that has to sit at a fixed URL.</p>
          <p class="takeaway">Every outside service has one address in your code.</p>
          <p class="see">See: <a href="/seams/">Seams</a></p>
        </div>
      </li>
      <li>
        <span class="n">05</span>
        <div>
          <h2>Make it act</h2>
          <p>Some of the most important work happens when nobody's clicking anything. Save a Lead and queue a follow-up job in the same transaction, so the job only runs if the save really happened. Caravan runs the queue and the cron schedule, right on SQLite. One notification can land in three places at once: in the app, live on screen, and in an inbox.</p>
          <p>Want an AI agent working in your app? MCP gives it exactly the permissions your gates already set, and nothing more.</p>
          <p class="home">In FrontierJS: Caravan · the transactional outbox · notifications · mcp. Orion, for triggers, conditions and actions, is being ported.</p>
          <p class="takeaway">The same rules apply to work nobody clicked.</p>
        </div>
      </li>
      <li>
        <span class="n">06</span>
        <div>
          <h2>Give it a place to live</h2>
          <p>Sooner or later your app has to run somewhere other than your laptop. That means config for each environment, secrets that stay out of the repo, ports that don't collide, and databases where the schema says they are. <code>$.config</code> reads your app's config and <code>app.configFor(tenant)</code> reads one tenant's. Every service gets its port from a scheme, so dev, test and prod never step on each other.</p>
          <p class="home">In FrontierJS: <code>junction.config.js</code> · <code>fli env:*</code> · the port scheme · <code>fli auth:rotate-key</code>.</p>
          <p class="takeaway">Where it runs is part of the design.</p>
        </div>
      </li>
      <li>
        <span class="n">07</span>
        <div>
          <h2>Keep watch</h2>
          <p>Once it's running, you need to know how it's doing. Every request carries a trace id. Health checks and metrics are registered right where the code lives, and the devtools panel shows calls as they happen. Outpost runs on each server, and Basecamp, itself a FrontierJS app, keeps an eye on the whole fleet.</p>
          <p class="home">In FrontierJS: Junction telemetry, health and devtools · Outpost · Basecamp · <code>fli deploy:logs</code>.</p>
          <p class="takeaway">Find out it's broken before a customer tells you.</p>
        </div>
      </li>
      <li>
        <span class="n">08</span>
        <div>
          <h2>Know what changed</h2>
          <p>Changing code shouldn't feel like defusing something. <code>createTestEnv</code> gives your tests a real database and a real API, built from the schema. Migrations come from diffing the schema, so there are no migration files to write by hand. <code>fli test:access</code> writes your whole access policy out as a snapshot, so if a change affects who can do what, you'll see it in the diff.</p>
          <p><code>fli proves</code> looks at what you changed and tells you which checks it needs. <code>fli done</code> tells you whether you're actually finished.</p>
          <p class="home">In FrontierJS: <code>createTestEnv</code> · <code>@frontierjs/testing</code> · <code>fli test:*</code> · generated snapshots.</p>
          <p class="takeaway">You can see what a change does before it ships.</p>
        </div>
      </li>
      <li>
        <span class="n">09</span>
        <div>
          <h2>Put it into the world</h2>
          <p>Time to ship. <code>fli release:mint</code> works out exactly what would deploy, without changing anything. <code>fli deploy</code> ships it, the deploy journal keeps track of every attempt, and if something goes wrong, <code>fli deploy:rollback</code> puts the previous release back.</p>
          <p><code>fli db:backup</code> takes a hot backup of every database the schema declares, and <code>fli db:import</code> pulls a copy of production down to your machine.</p>
          <p class="home">One gap: restoring a backup into production is still a manual step.</p>
          <p class="takeaway">Shipping is repeatable, and so is undoing it.</p>
        </div>
      </li>
      <li>
        <span class="n">10</span>
        <div>
          <h2>Build the builder</h2>
          <p>The tools you work in every day are part of the system too. <code>fli</code> runs commands written in markdown, so your project's own commands live right next to the framework's. The editor extension understands <code>.lite</code> and <code>.mesa</code>. <code>fli check</code> turns your project's conventions into checks that actually fail. And an AI agent helping with your code reads the same map you do.</p>
          <p class="home">In FrontierJS: <code>fli</code> · <code>create-frontier</code> · <code>fli check</code> · the VS Code extension (unpublished).</p>
          <p class="takeaway">Your tools follow the same rules as your app.</p>
        </div>
      </li>
      <li class="reveal">
        <span class="n">11</span>
        <div>
          <h2>One system, many views</h2>
          <p>Remember the <code>@@gate</code> on Lead? Here's everywhere it shows up, and you only wrote it once:</p>
          <table class="views">
            <tbody>
              <tr><th>Data</th><td>the query refuses rows the caller can't see</td></tr>
              <tr><th>API</th><td>a denied call gets its status from the one place that maps errors</td></tr>
              <tr><th>UI</th><td><code>x-gate</code> hides the button, and the server enforces the rule either way</td></tr>
              <tr><th>Agent</th><td>the tool isn't listed for a caller who can't use it</td></tr>
              <tr><th>Proof</th><td><code>fli test:access</code> puts the change in the diff</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
          <p>That's the whole idea in three parts: <strong>one origin, one name, one owner.</strong> Every fact starts in one place, every thing has one name, and every job has one place that does it.</p>
          <p class="takeaway">Predictability is the product.</p>
        </div>
      </li>
      <li>
        <span class="n">12</span>
        <div>
          <h2>Leave a map</h2>
          <p>Code tells you what. It doesn't tell you why. The reasons go in <code>DECISIONS.md</code>, what's broken goes in <code>ISSUES.md</code>, and what isn't built yet goes in <code>IDEAS/</code>. <code>fli</code> reads all three together: <code>fli next</code> suggests what to work on, <code>fli file</code> logs a new issue, and <code>fli decide</code> records a decision. Basecamp, the biggest app built on FrontierJS, is the worked example.</p>
          <p class="takeaway">The next person reading this might be you, six months from now.</p>
          <p class="see">See: <a href="/map/">Map</a> · <a href="/landscape/">Landscape</a> · <a href="/journey/">Journey</a></p>
        </div>
      </li>
    </ol>
  </div>
</section>

